Date: Thu, 20 May 93 22:25:11 -0400
From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Subject: AI: Boolean Dream...

Peter McCluskey writes:
> I want to store the knowledge and intelligence contained within the mind,
>and don't care if some quirks caused by the biological limits of the existing
>implementation of intelligence are lost. You apparently want more biological
>realism than I do. I admit that I can't provide a compelling argument for
>my assumptions about how the important information is stored; they are
>educated guesses or intuitions based on many little pieces of information
>which I can't enumerate.

You are apparently making the assumption that the knowledge and intelligence
contained in the mind is easily seperable from the biological processes
that implement it.  This is reasonable, and many people make the same
assumption, but I'm not one of them.  For this to be true the brain would
have to have some kind of explicit code for information storage, a
"language" of sorts.  This does not have to be the case, it's probably just
wishful thinking on our part, supported only by our own technological
solutions.  All neurons need to do is enable processes that result in an
adaptive behavior.  For an external observer, it may be convenient to
describe this as "recall of stored memories", but the information might only
be implicitly coded, and only decodable through its implementation in a
behavioral context...

>>Anyway, synaptic "weights" are presumably learned by a Hebbian correlation, an
>>assumption most people accept.  However, the mechanism for the Hebbian synapse
>>has still not been found (though recent findings suggest that carbon monoxide,
>>of all things, may be involved...)

> Don't you mean nitric oxide? Not that that is any less surprising.

Actually, both nitric oxide and carbon monoxide are currently prime
candidates for the "retrograde messenger" that is necessary to implement
a Hebbian synapse.  Perhaps the notion is so attractive precisely because
it is so ironic...

Eric Watt Forste writes:
>You'll hate this, I'm sure, but I'm skeptical about the possibility of
>designing a thinking machine without complex sensory apparatus. Have
>you ever read Doug Hofstadter's essay "Waking up from the Boolean
>Dream" in Scientific American? It's republished in his book
>Metamagical Themas. Highly recommended.

I agree completely.  I haven't read the book you recommend, but this
opinion is becoming increasingly accepted.  Some people maintain that
an organism must be studied as an entire system - complete with perception
and action, and even an environmental framework.  Consider a seperate
visual system - what is its output?  I suggest that the only reasonable
answer is a motor response...

Paul
 

