Date: Fri, 21 May 93 00:46:45 -0700
From: Rebecca Crowley <selene@blegga.cpac.washington.edu>
Subject: BOOK (s) : Mild extropianism in sf novels

M.A. Foster, sf author of a variety of books exploring sociological
issues (including some interesting depictions of various family
structures) wrote a book called _Preserver_ (third in a series, 
the other two being _The Morphodite_ and _Transformer_).  I mention
this because the background society in this novel is arguably
Extropian in nature, with something of a dark side to it.  That
is, the planet(oid) is described has having no government, but instead
is controlled (bad word, I know) by a variety of organizations, which 
provide employement, protection, etc.  Most people wind up joining
one of these organizations, which provide education as well, at an early
age and more or less stick with that group (occasionally jumping ship to
a competitor).  The protagonist is a freelancer, selling services
(various -- intelligence work, a variety of other things) to some
of these organizations.  Our Hero has, of course, a variety of
interesting powers, depicted in the previous books as well (they're
actually kind of cool -- they involve a technique for making
connections between seemingly unconnected data and using the resulting
understanding to effect global change with very minute but carefully
considered actions.  I'm not sure I'd go so far as to recommend this
novel (after all, one does have to slog through the first two to
really understand the third) -- the prose style is not the greatest
and takes some getting used to.  Also, they've been OOP for a while,
and were only ever DAW paperbacks, anyway, so you might have to dig
around a bit.  But it's a very well thought out depiction of 
anarchocapitalism, if not all that flattering.

I finally got around to reading Neal Stephenson's _Snow Crash_.
As a lot of people posted about this book before, I see little
point in going into any detail.  I did enjoy it, despite the odd prose
style (funny reading all those verbs in the present tense.  I kept
seeing this book as a movie in my head) and mildly ridiculous
nlp underpinnings.  On a totally non-list relevant, but nevertheless 
related tangent, Mr. Stephenson was at the local University Book Store 
last week for a signing party (along with a bunch of other local sf authors).  
I asked him where I might be able to find his first novel (I have Zodiac, 
altho I haven't read it yet).  He said, "Don't bother, it's terrible.  
That's what first novels are for."  :-)  

As for this Chaitin topic, you wouldn't believe what a shock I got
reading the first couple of posts.  See, my exposure to Chaitin so
far has been via my job (C/C++ compilers).  I've been hacking away
on a global register allocator, using a modified version of Chaitin's 
algorithm, and just wasn't *expecting* computational theory to rear its 
lovely head. 

And as long as I'm posting on semi-random discussions, I'll add
my $.02 to the 'stumbling across' micro-thread.  As has been
pointed out, hanging out with people with similar interests
and, in particular, raiding their libraries, will certainly
increase the odds of running into cool stuff.  But there's no
underestimating the kind of non-coincidental apparent coincidence
that starts to occur regularly if you hang out on electronic boards
(esp. Usenet).  I'm convinced I buzz by recommendations/references
to books all the time, only to pick stuff up "randomly" at bookstores,
and remember belatedly where I heard about them.  There's  just
something funny about information saturation.

