Amara Graps writes:

> Sveiks, (Latvian for hi)
> 
> I've been more or less following the Chaitin thread. I haven't read any of 
> the pointed-at sources, but I did find another source that may be useful to 
> some of you. The book is Rudy Rucker's  _Mind Tools_. 
> 
> Yesterday evening, Tim May  mentioned that Chaitin refers to Rucker's book in 
> one of his (Chaitin's) books, but because Tim had misplaced it, he couldn't 
> look up  some of the references to facilitate this active discussion.  So I 
> pulled it off my boyfriend's bookshelf this morning and started thumbing 
> through it.

And even today, I was reading the very same section at the Stanford
Bookstore, because my copy is still misplaced. It was this book I read
in the spring of 1987 that alerted me to the significance of Chaitin's
approach, especially as it seemed to deal naturally with issues
of complexity in life, strings, and the power of scientific theories.
(Cover and Thomas, amongst others, view Shannon's information theory
as a subset of Kolmogoroff-Chaitin complexity, and Occam's razor is
naturally translatable into algorithmic complexity terms.)

Anyway, Rucker's discussin of Chaitin sparked my interest. (Someone
who may be on the Extropians list, Jed Harris, had told be about
Chaitin just before I left Intel in 1986, but I missed the real
significance then.) A few months later, in September of 1987, Chaitin
was supposed to talk on this set of ideas at the A-LIFE conference. As
it turned out, Chaitin cancelled.  But, ironically, I had a great
discussion with Rudy Rucker on these topics, and told him how much I
admired "Mind Tools" (I think I admired it so much I lent it out to
someone, which is why it's now missing!).  That same night I also had
dinner in Santa Fe with Richard Dawkins, Keith Henson and Arel Lucas, and Hans
Moravec, amongst others, and then rode back up to Los Alamos with John
Markoff, Kevin Kelly, and Rucker.) 

(This first A-LIFE conference only had about 100 attendees, but it was
really dense-packed with exciting speakers and ideas. John Holland
(genetic algorithms), Richard Dawkins (first demo of his "biomorphs"),
A. Lindenmayer (L-systems), Drexler (nanotech, of course), Mark Miller
(didn't give a talk), Hans Moravac (robots), and lots of others
scientists, writers (James Gleich, about to hit it big with "Chaos"),
John Markoff, Kevin Kelly, who wrote up the conference for "WER", Fred
Hapgood, and so on. Several Extropians, too.)

At the 1989 Hackers Conference, Rucker and I talked again about math
and Godel, and Marc Stiegler and Vernor Vinge were present, but I
can't recall what, if any, their reaction was. (For you interested
folks, Stiegler read aloud in a moonlit grove his newest story, "The
Gentle Seduction."  Vinge gave some tantalyzing hints about his work
in progress and asked some good questions about public key crypto and
the new work on "zero knowledge systems." He asked us how we liked the
title "A Tire Upon the Heap"...we urged him to change it (just kidding
about this part).)

Anyway, I will not comment line by line on Amara's excellent posting,
even though it is filled with "arguments by authority." ("grin") Some
very nice explanations by Rucker...who is quite a good
metamathematician in many ways, and who had rare interviews with Kurt
Godel just before Godel's death in 1978...those who know RR chiefly
through SF, "Mondo 2000," and AutoDesk (Chaos and CAs), should check
out his math books.

And, by the way, more "arguments from authority" ("grin") are to found
in Barrow's "Pi in the Sky," an excellent examination of math and its
relation to reality published last year and not yet out in paperback.
Barrow--the same Barrow as in the anthropic cosmological
principle--devotes a section to the consequences for the Platonist
model of reality implied by Chaitin's work. (The same point I was
makin when I jumped in and cited Chaitin as _support_ for one of
Raymond's conlusions about Platonist realities, ironically enough.)

So, a wealth of material at all levels, on this thread. To sum them
up:

* Light introductions: Barrow's "Pi in the Sky," Davies' "The Mind of
God," and Casti's "Searching for Certainty." The "New Scientist"
article, "A Random Walk in Arithmetic," 24 March 1990.

(and the concepts of Universal Turing Machines, the halting problem,
logic, etc., are covered in various books, including Hofstadter's
justly famed "Godel, Escher, Bach.")

* Meatier introductions: the last chapter in Rucker's "Mind Tools."
(Though earlier chapters are needed as preparation.). Also, the
"Scientific American" article mentioned several times (August 1988).

* Detailed and Technical treatments: Chaitin's three books, beginning
with "Information, Randomness, and Incompleteness." Herken's "The
Universal Turing Machine: A Half Century Survey," contains many
readable (though advanced) papers by logicians and computer
scientists, including Chaitin and Bennett. Ditto for the
many-times-mentioned on reversible logic and entropy.


Though Mike Price may again claim that I am somehow belittling him (or
others), let me just say again that these areas of math--Godel's
Theorem, computatibility, Turing Machines, randomness, complexity, the
nature of proof, the halting problem, oracles, etc.--are notoriously
complex and subject to misinterpretation. Most of the terms, e.g.,
"inputs accepted by a UTM," have precise meanings which may or may not
match ordinary intuititions and semantics--EVEN AMONGST SO-CALLED
EXPERTS!. Hofstadter devoted a huge chunk of his huge book to
introducing the various terms needed (including some he coined
himself, such as "strange loops," because the terms are so fraught
with meaning), as have Smullyan, Chaitin, and others who have written
explanations.

This issue of complexity, randomness, logical depth, and "knowability"
is, I think, of incredible importance to the Extropian outlook. It
also impinges on issues of genetic programming (the strings of Koza's
evolving units are increasing in logical depth), on the nature of
intelligence (can a system like a mind be "compressed" to a much
shorter description than itself, e.g., simple laws of thought?), and
on epistemological theories.

I intend to keep writing on it. (I posted several essays last fall on
logical depth, Bennett's use of Chaitin's work, and reversible
computations.)

Cheers.

-Tim



