  One tack you could take would be to ask him if he knows what Septra,
propranolol, dextran sulfate, aspirin, penicillin, digitalis and 
fluroxene are.  
 
  " ... according to George Hitchings, co-winner of the 1988 Nobel
prize in medicine, FDA's five-year delay in approving the anti-bacterial
drug Septra cost 80,000 lives."  [1]
                     -- Sam Kazman, Competitive Enterprise Institute

  The FDA caused a 3 year delay (1965-67) of propranolol (a beta-blocker
drug to treat angina and hypertension), causing a loss of 30,000 lives 
during that period [2].  In 1968, the FDA approved it only for minor
uses, and they banned advertising it as a treatment for angina (until
1973) and for hypertension (until 1976), so many more Americans died
during that period since their doctors hesitated to prescribe an
"unapproved" drug.

  Customs routinely confiscated dextran sulfate from AIDS patients [3]
until July 1988 when FDA Commissioner Frank Young apparently thought
it politically wise to grant them access to a potentially beneficial
drug.  At that time, it was known that d.s. prevented HIV from infecting
white blood cells in vitro [4], but the FDA had kept it from being sold
in the US until it was "proven to work."  Needless to say, many AIDS 
patients didn't have the time to wait around until testing was done.

  I believe that there was a similar (class of) drug(s) that had the
potential to help Alzheimer patients, and that it was banned by the 
FDA until recently when political opportunism seemed wise ... but I
don't know the name of that drug.  Perhaps someone else here does?

  Tim mentioned that aspirin and penicillin wouldn't pass current FDA 
requirements (aspirin deforms fetuses of almost every mammalian 
species except humans [5], and even penicillin has killed people who
were allergic to it [6]).  You can add to that list the useful
drugs digitalis and fluroxene. [7]

  "The failure to approve an important new drug can be as detrimental
to the public health as the approval of a potentially bad drug."  [8]
                        -- Alexander Schmidt, former FDA Commissioner
  "Many more lives have probably been lost by the aggression of
[pharmaceutical] licensing laws than have been saved." -- Dr. Ruwart [9]

  The FDA requires manufacturers to perform many years of testing with
an average cost of $200 million per drug [10], and then the FDA takes
an average of 2.5 years to decide if "enough" testing has been done! 
[11].  This causes enormous economic pressure on firms to focus only 
on drugs which can be used widely (and to ignore cures for comparatively
rarer diseases), to totally abandon unpatentable treatments (e.g.,
vitamin/mineral therapies), and to raise their prices through the roof
(as Perry mentioned) so they can recoup their losses while they have a 
patent-monopoly on the drug.

  Dr. Ruwart strongly opposes the FDA "Drug Lag" and argues that its
"functions" could be entirely taken over and improved by the market.
Professional groups (like the AMA's Seal of Approval program) and 
consumer groups (like the old "Consumer's Research" or Perry's 
Underwriters (Drug) Laboratories) could test drugs in their own labs,
and could be funded by: concerned citizens, organization membership,
manufacturer fees, and selling the information to people for small change.
The profits to be gained would encourage testing and reporting by several 
independent groups, such as the "Medical Letter on Drugs and Therapies"
which is supported totally through subscriptions to doctors, pharmacists,
pharmaceutical manufacturers, and medical students.

