Subject: Chaitin, Randomness, and Explanations on this List

Dan Davis:

> >  no legal program can be
> >  the prefix of another, and there are fewer than 2^n programs
> >  with n bits.
> 
> Are you sure? There would have to be a good deal less than 2^n programs
> with n bits to make this a distribution.
> 
> Do they prove that their restricted set of legal programs 
> satisfies some condition so that SUM converges?

Well, I can't find such a proof in any of the material that I have.
To those guys, it's probably obvious.  Let me try!

-------------------------------

Let S be a set of binary strings, none on which is the prefix of
another.  Let |n| be the length of string n.  Then:

  THEOREM:  

                       |n|
             SUM   (1/2)     <=  1      [ That is, Omega is l.t. or = 1, if ]
            n in S                      [ S is the set of halting programs  ]
  
  PROOF:

    Let m = MAX( |n| ), for n in S.  Because no two strings in S
    are prefixes of each other, none of them are prefixes of the
    same string of length m.  Now, each of the strings n in S
                      (m - |n|)         m
    is the prefix of 2          of the 2  strings of length m.
    So, now we have:
                                (m - |n|)       m
                         SUM   2           <=  2
                        n in S
               m
    Divide by 2 :      
                                 (m - |n|)
                                2
                         SUM   -----------     <=  1
                        n in S      m
                                   2
    
    Gives:
                       |n|
             SUM   (1/2)     <=  1
            n in S

    Q.E.D.

