From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Is Math Random?

Robin writes (on a thread that may actually be winding down!):

> But I can't see how it helps to conclude that math
> is more random than anything else.

We seem to have reached agreement.  What I found pleasing and
interesting (perhaps not "revolutionary", but what's that?) was
that such randomness is there in number theory at all.  OK, so
math isn't more random than other random things -- to me there
were always two sorts of randomness:  That caused by physical
fundamentally random processes (if there are any), i.e. quantum
mechanics; and that caused by lack of knowledge (chaotic dynamics,
e.g. coin tossing, the weather).

The former doesn't make sense to apply to number theory, and the
latter shouldn't either since we have as many axioms as we want
and their abstract deductive closure.  And yet here's this random
Omega.  Kinda cool.

Thanks to all who goaded me into grinding away at the material;
through such episodes does growth occur.

derek


