Date: Fri, 21 May 1993 14:03:35 -0500
From: extr@jido.b30.ingr.com (Craig Presson)
Subject: REVIEW: _Nanotechnology Playhouse_

[Review prepared by Freeman Craig Presson for the Extropians list; may
be echoed in _Exponent_ and on sci.nanotech if desired]

Christopher Lampton, _Nanotechnology Playhouse: building machines from
atoms_, Waite Group Press, Corte Madera, California 1993; pb, 131pp +
5.15" multimedia demo disk by Phillip Shaddock. US$23.95.

Wowie-Zowie and rah-rah! This book makes me feel simultaneously elated
and scared. A very slick exposition of molecular nanotech which a
bright about-ten-year-old could understand and enjoy (I happen to have
a supply of those at home, so I plan to test this statement), it
covers an amazing proportion of the material in Drexler's popular
books _Engines of Creation_ and _Unbounding the Future_.

Drexler and Chris Peterson reviewed the book for technical errors, a
step for which I commend the Waite group. Anyone who has read
_Engines_ or _Unbounding_ will not find much new here; however, the
writing is sufficiently entertaining and clever, livened up with
occasional "future look-aside" vignettes and observations from the
conferences and articles that have happened since _Engines_, not to
forget the really classy grayscale illustrations, that even those
well-read in nano-literature might find it worth buying.  Mentioning
the literature brings me to the one glaring production problem with
_Playhouse_: the bibliography, mentioned in the book and in the
catalog entry, appears to have vanished without explanation. I found
some references (including one to _Nanosystems_) included _passim_ in
necessarily abbreviated form, and Drexler manages to give the
Foresight Institute's address in his Foreword (who else would write
the Foreword? If you can't get Drexler, Merkle, or maybe JoSH, you
just leave it out and hope no one notices); but having at least the
basic suggestions for further reading would increase the book's
usefulness.

I found the nanodemo nicely done; I won't say too much about it
because I haven't run it on a decent machine (e.g., my home box) yet.
It has sound if and only if you have a real Soundblaster card. Mouse
support didn't seem to work on the surplus '386 I borrowed to try it
at work -- remember that I needed a 5.25" floppy drive (complain
complain) -- therefore I could not run all of the program.

Why feel scared by such a book? Because I see it as entirely too
devoted to cheerleading. Even though the author included some
disclaimers, and a short discussion of downside issues, _Playhouse_
manages to make everything look too easy and too inevitable, and may
end up acting for my children like the comic books that dealt with
"Mars colonies by 1990" did for people my age: as a source of both
vision and frustration, and a temptation to intellectual _hubris_. Of
course most Extropians probably don't share the common assessment of
intellectual _hubris_ as a problem. Professing life goals like living
forever, incorporating the Universe into one's mind, and recursively
evolving into _Brahma_ tends to normalize _hubris_.

Other similar books are available from the Waite Group, including
_Artificial Life Playhouse_ and _Virtual Reality Playhouse_. I already
own the one on fractals which includes Winfract 17.50; it has provided
a lot of fun in an inexpensive package.

I have no connection to the Waite Group or the authors, etc.

